<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows + canRetrieveWindowContent let the service scan getWindows() so a
     budgeted app is caught in ANY visible pane (split-screen / Samsung App Pairs), not just the
     focused window. typeWindowsChanged fires when panes appear/disappear without a focus change.
     typeWindowContentChanged exists ONLY for the settings-watch: navigation inside the Settings app
     doesn't reliably fire a window-state change, so content changes are scanned too - the handler
     drops them immediately for every non-Settings package.

     flagIncludeNotImportantViews is what makes the Instagram surface rule work at all (Gate B
     2026-07-23). Without it the framework hands us only views that are "important for accessibility"
     - clickable / focusable / labelled - and silently promotes the children of everything else. But
     the reel signal we key on, clips_viewer_view_pager, is a bare ViewPager: text="", content-desc="",
     clickable=false, focusable=false (only scrollable=true, which does NOT count). So it was never in
     our tree, targetFor() always saw a free surface, and reels never blocked.
     The whole ig-dumps/MAPPING.md rule was derived from `uiautomator dump`, and uiautomator's own
     UiAutomation sets this flag - so without it here, dumps and the live service see different trees
     and every id-based mapping is unsound. Keep the two in sync. Cost is small: the full reel tree is
     ~120 nodes (see ig_reel_v439_gateB-dump.xml), well inside the walk budgets.

     flagReportViewIds is the other half of the same Gate-B bug, and on its own is fatal:
     AccessibilityNodeInfo.getViewIdResourceName() returns NULL unless the service declares it. Every
     resource-id this app keys on - the Instagram reel signals AND the Chrome/Brave url_bar - would
     read as null, i.e. "no reel, no URL", blocking nothing. Note which features were verified on
     hardware before this: settings-watch (matches on *text*) and TikTok/X (match on *package*).
     Nothing that ever worked on-device depended on a resource id; everything that did was broken.
     Confirmed on the S25: `dumpsys accessibility` showed fetchFlags=128 - the include-not-important
     bit alone, with no report-view-ids bit beside it.

     isAccessibilityTool is the third member of that family - declared config whose absence fails
     silently, and the newest. Android 17's Advanced Protection Mode revokes the accessibility
     permission from every service NOT declaring it, and blocks re-granting while the mode is on;
     the exempted set is "accessibility tools", and Google names automation / launcher / customization
     apps as the ones it intends to cut off. Without this line, one toggle (Settings > Google >
     Advanced Protection) switches the blocker off for good - a bypass that needs no key, no computer
     and no waiting, and that the settings-watch cannot see because that page belongs to Google Play
     services, not to App-Block. Declared true because this IS the app's function: it reads window
     content and acts on it as the user's own agent. Attribute added in API 31, so it is inert on
     older devices and costs nothing there.
     One UI 9 / Android 17 reached stable 2026-07-22 and the S25 FE is in beta, so this is live.
     NOT independently verified: whether the manifest flag alone satisfies AAPM, or Google also
     consults a server-side list. Every published source names only the attribute.

     flagRequestAccessibilityButton closes the fourth silent door (audit 2026-08-21, N-1), and unlike
     the three above it is not about what the service *sees* but about who can switch it OFF. In
     AccessibilityManagerService.performAccessibilityShortcutTargetService (AOSP, read at the line),
     a service whose targetSdk is above Q and which does NOT request the accessibility button is
     TOGGLED by every accessibility shortcut: connected -> disableAccessibilityServiceLocked. That
     covers the volume-key chord, the floating accessibility button, the Quick Settings tile and
     the key gesture alike. Entry on One UI is Settings > Accessibility > Advanced settings > pick a
     shortcut > Select actions > tick "App-Block detection" - a list page, which the settings-watch
     correctly leaves alone - and from then on the chord turns the blocker off in under two seconds
     with no key, no computer, no reboot, and the same chord turns it back on before the watchdog's
     15-minute nag. With this flag the same method takes the other branch: the HARDWARE shortcut
     can only ENABLE a disabled service, and every other shortcut type becomes an
     onAccessibilityButtonClicked callback, which this service leaves as the no-op default. Added in
     API 26, so it is live on every supported device.

     VERIFIED on the S25 2026-08-29, and the verification corrected this comment. The mechanism holds -
     `dumpsys accessibility` shows requestAccessibilityButton=true and no shortcut toggles the service
     off - but the sentence that used to stand here, "no floating button appears because of this line",
     was wrong, and wrong in the direction that matters. One UI adds the service to
     `accessibility_button_targets` ON INSTALL, unasked; with gesture navigation it then forces
     `accessibility_button_mode=1` and draws a floating pill. Long-press that pill -> Edit -> untick
     "App-Block detection" and `enabled_accessibility_services` is EMPTY: blocking off, four taps, no
     key, no computer, and nothing notices because the watchdog runs inside the service that just died.
     Confirmed twice.

     So N-1 traded a chord for a pill. The pill's picker is now bounced by SettingsWatch's checkable
     rule (see Screen.checkables - our label sits ON the CheckBox there, where every list puts a bare
     switch beside a label), and the Lock tab names the claim out loud, because clearing the target
     itself needs `adb shell settings put secure accessibility_button_targets ""` and comes back on the
     next install. Removing this flag is NOT the fix: it reopens the chord, which is worse. -->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowsChanged|typeWindowContentChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows|flagIncludeNotImportantViews|flagReportViewIds|flagRequestAccessibilityButton"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="100"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_description" />
